  "raw_response": "def solve(grid):\n    # find dimensions\n    rows = len(grid)\n    cols = len(grid[0]) if rows else 0\n\n    # locate the four non\u2011zero cells\n    points = []\n    for r in range(rows):\n        for c in range(cols):\n            if grid[r][c] != 0:\n                points.append((r, c, grid[r][c]))\n\n    # there should be exactly four coloured cells\n    # order them by row\n    points.sort(key=lambda x: x[0])\n    r1 = points[0][0]\n    r2 = points[2][0]   # because rows are increasing\n    # within each row find leftmost and rightmost column\n    row0 = [(c, v) for (r, c, v) in points if r == r1]\n    row1 = [(c, v) for (r, c, v) in points if r == r2]\n    row0.sort()\n    row1.sort()\n    c1, valA = row0[0]          # top\u2011left value\n    c2, valB = row0[1]          # top\u2011right value\n    # bottom row must contain the swapped values\n    # (not needed for the algorithm, but we keep the mapping)\n    opposite = {valA: valB, valB: valA}\n\n    # initialise output with zeros\n    out = [[0] * cols for _ in range(rows)]\n\n    # ----- 3x3 blocks -----\n    # helper to fill a block around a centre (r,c) with centre value v\n    def fill_block(r, c, v):\n        opp = opposite[v]\n        for dr in (-1, 0, 1):\n            for dc in (-1, 0, 1):\n                nr, nc = r + dr, c + dc\n                if 0 <= nr < rows and 0 <= nc < cols:\n                    out[nr][nc] = v if dr == 0 and dc == 0 else opp\n\n    # four corners\n    fill_block(r1, c1, valA)          # top\u2011left\n    fill_block(r1, c2, valB)          # top\u2011right\n    fill_block(r2, c1, valB)          # bottom\u2011left\n    fill_block(r2, c2, valA)          # bottom\u2011right\n\n    # ----- 5's on the edges of the rectangle -----\n    # top and bottom edges (row r1 and r2)\n    for c in range(c1 + 2, c2):          # c1+2 \u2026 c2-2\n        dist = min(c - (c1 + 1), (c2 - 1) - c)\n        if dist % 2 == 1:\n            out[r1][c] = 5\n            out[r2][c] = 5\n\n    # left and right edges (column c1 and c2)\n    for r in range(r1 + 2, r2):          # r1+2 \u2026 r2-2\n        dist = min(r - (r1 + 1), (r2 - 1) - r)\n        if dist % 2 == 1:\n            out[r][c1] = 5\n            out[r][c2] = 5\n\n    return out",
  "script": "def solve(grid):\n    # find dimensions\n    rows = len(grid)\n    cols = len(grid[0]) if rows else 0\n\n    # locate the four non\u2011zero cells\n    points = []\n    for r in range(rows):\n        for c in range(cols):\n            if grid[r][c] != 0:\n                points.append((r, c, grid[r][c]))\n\n    # there should be exactly four coloured cells\n    # order them by row\n    points.sort(key=lambda x: x[0])\n    r1 = points[0][0]\n    r2 = points[2][0]   # because rows are increasing\n    # within each row find leftmost and rightmost column\n    row0 = [(c, v) for (r, c, v) in points if r == r1]\n    row1 = [(c, v) for (r, c, v) in points if r == r2]\n    row0.sort()\n    row1.sort()\n    c1, valA = row0[0]          # top\u2011left value\n    c2, valB = row0[1]          # top\u2011right value\n    # bottom row must contain the swapped values\n    # (not needed for the algorithm, but we keep the mapping)\n    opposite = {valA: valB, valB: valA}\n\n    # initialise output with zeros\n    out = [[0] * cols for _ in range(rows)]\n\n    # ----- 3x3 blocks -----\n    # helper to fill a block around a centre (r,c) with centre value v\n    def fill_block(r, c, v):\n        opp = opposite[v]\n        for dr in (-1, 0, 1):\n            for dc in (-1, 0, 1):\n                nr, nc = r + dr, c + dc\n                if 0 <= nr < rows and 0 <= nc < cols:\n                    out[nr][nc] = v if dr == 0 and dc == 0 else opp\n\n    # four corners\n    fill_block(r1, c1, valA)          # top\u2011left\n    fill_block(r1, c2, valB)          # top\u2011right\n    fill_block(r2, c1, valB)          # bottom\u2011left\n    fill_block(r2, c2, valA)          # bottom\u2011right\n\n    # ----- 5's on the edges of the rectangle -----\n    # top and bottom edges (row r1 and r2)\n    for c in range(c1 + 2, c2):          # c1+2 \u2026 c2-2\n        dist = min(c - (c1 + 1), (c2 - 1) - c)\n        if dist % 2 == 1:\n            out[r1][c] = 5\n            out[r2][c] = 5\n\n    # left and right edges (column c1 and c2)\n    for r in range(r1 + 2, r2):          # r1+2 \u2026 r2-2\n        dist = min(r - (r1 + 1), (r2 - 1) - r)\n        if dist % 2 == 1:\n            out[r][c1] = 5\n            out[r][c2] = 5\n\n    return out",
